From 1b741a5326d6a7d251fdd9270ce482d480f88c6f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Witold=20Bo=C5=82t?= <witold.bolt@jit.team>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 03:03:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] gio: add a polling local file monitor for systems without
 notification

On a kernel with no file change notification at all (no inotify, kqueue
or FEN -- Phoenix-RTOS) GIO has no GLocalFileMonitor implementation, so
g_file_monitor_directory() and g_file_monitor_file() on a local path fail
with "Unable to find default local file monitor type". File managers,
desktops and panels then never see a file appear, disappear or change.
GPollFileMonitor does not help: it is used only for single non-local
files.

Add a "poll" backend, selected by -Dfile_monitor_backend=poll and by
"auto" when none of the others is available, and registered at priority
10 (inotify is 20) so that a real backend still wins.

A file is stat()ed every tick. A directory is stat()ed every tick, and
its entries are read (one readdir per entry and a message to a
filesystem server for each, on such systems) only when that stat
changed: POSIX requires link(), unlink() and rename() to update the
directory's st_mtime and st_ctime, so while they, its inode, size and
link count stay as they were, no entry was added, removed or renamed.
The Phoenix-RTOS filesystems a desktop runs from all do: ext2 (what
stat() returns always moved; since filesystems 5c807d1 "ext2: POSIX
timestamps on link/unlink; write directory changes back" the on-disk
copy does too), the NFS client (the server's own stamps, through a
100 ms attribute cache that its own changes drop) and dummyfs (mtime).

Timestamps have a granularity -- one second on Phoenix-RTOS, whose
stat() leaves tv_nsec 0, two seconds on FAT -- so an entry added in the
same granule as the read before it leaves them unchanged. A listing is
trusted to stand for the directory only if it was read at least 2 s
after the monitor first saw the directory's current stamps; until then
the entries are read on every tick, which costs each change two more
reads. The 2 s are measured on the monotonic clock rather than by
comparing the mtime with the time of day, which on an NFS root would
compare the clocks of two machines.

A filesystem that does not update a directory's stamps is detected, not
assumed (statfs() cannot tell the filesystems apart): the sweep below
reads the entries too, and when it finds names added or removed under
stamps that did not move outside the window, that monitor reads the
entries on every tick from then on, as before. Only that first change
is reported late, by up to one sweep period.

Content and attribute changes of the entries do not touch the
directory, so a sweep lstat()s every entry: every 4 ticks while the
directory changes, backing off (doubling, up to every 32 ticks) while
sweeps find nothing; any event brings it back to 4. lstat() of a path
is the expensive call (on Phoenix-RTOS libc two messages per path
component), so this is now most of an idle monitor's cost. Large
directories are polled and swept less often still, so that the cost of
one monitor stays bounded.

The poller is a GSource on GLib's worker context, as the notification
backends' readers are, so that synchronous stat() calls -- network
round trips on an NFS root -- never stall the application's main loop;
the events reach the monitor's context through the thread-safe
g_file_monitor_source_handle_event(). The tick is GIO_POLL_INTERVAL_MS
(100..60000 ms, default 1000).
